  "Question": "Given \\(a<0\\), what does \\(\\sqrt{a^2}\\) equal?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-a\\)",
    "B": "\\(a\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\pm a\\)",
    "D": "\\(a^2\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(\\sqrt{a^2}=|a|\\). When \\(a<0\\), \\(|a|=-a\\), so the result is \\(-a\\) (which is positive).\n\nAlternative method 1: Use the principal-root definition\n\nSince \\(a<0\\), the number \\(-a\\) is positive and satisfies \\((-a)^2=a^2\\). The principal square root is the nonnegative square root, so \\(\\sqrt{a^2}=-a\\), choice A.",
